WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.533
A clock has two hands, and each
one tells you something different.

2
00:00:08.753 --> 00:00:12.654
Read them properly and you can
catch a bus, or time a programme.

3
00:00:13.154 --> 00:00:16.637
Time does not count in
tens. It counts in sixties.

4
00:00:16.857 --> 00:00:20.990
Sixty seconds make one minute,
and sixty minutes make one hour.

5
00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:25.715
Twenty-four hours make one whole
day, from midnight to midnight.

6
00:00:25.935 --> 00:00:30.254
Four hours is four lots of sixty
minutes, which is 240 minutes.

7
00:00:30.474 --> 00:00:36.929
To go back the other way, you
divide. 240 divided by 60 is 4 hours.

8
00:00:37.429 --> 00:00:42.166
Here is a clock. The short hand and
the long hand do different jobs.

9
00:00:42.386 --> 00:00:46.751
The short hand gives the hour.
It is sitting between 4 and 5.

10
00:00:46.971 --> 00:00:51.104
Take the hour it has passed.
So the hour is 4, not 5.

11
00:00:51.324 --> 00:00:56.386
The long hand gives the minutes. Every
number on the face is five minutes.

12
00:00:56.606 --> 00:01:01.157
It points at the 8, and eight
fives are 40. The time is 4:40.

13
00:01:01.657 --> 00:01:06.812
A timetable never says a.m. or
p.m. It uses 24-hour time instead.

14
00:01:07.032 --> 00:01:12.373
Morning hours stay as they are.
Quarter past nine is written 09:15.

15
00:01:12.593 --> 00:01:17.655
After midday, add 12 to the hour.
Half past three becomes 15:30.

16
00:01:17.875 --> 00:01:23.122
So 15:45 is 3:45 in the
afternoon. Take the 12 off again.

17
00:01:23.342 --> 00:01:27.522
Midday is written 12:00. Midnight
is written with four zeros.

18
00:01:28.022 --> 00:01:32.527
This is a real bus timetable,
and every time on it is 24-hour.

19
00:01:32.747 --> 00:01:36.369
Read down a column to follow
one bus all the way to school.

20
00:01:36.589 --> 00:01:40.536
Read across a row to see when
every bus reaches that one stop.

21
00:01:40.756 --> 00:01:45.586
Hodan must be at school by nine
o'clock. Which is the latest bus?

22
00:01:45.806 --> 00:01:52.819
Bus C does not arrive until 10:40, far
too late. Bus B gets there at 08:55.

23
00:01:53.319 --> 00:01:57.127
How long did something last? Count
on from the start to the end.

24
00:01:57.347 --> 00:02:00.969
The programme starts at
10:40 and ends at 12:15.

25
00:02:01.189 --> 00:02:05.740
Count on 20 minutes to 11:00,
then one whole hour to 12:00.

26
00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:10.743
Then 15 minutes more. That is 1
hour and 35 minutes altogether.

27
00:02:10.963 --> 00:02:16.815
Never take the times away like ordinary
numbers. An hour is 60 minutes.

28
00:02:17.315 --> 00:02:21.773
Days work the same way. It is
28 April, and you add 9 days.

29
00:02:21.993 --> 00:02:25.244
April has 30 days. There is no 37 April.

30
00:02:25.464 --> 00:02:28.947
Two days take you to the
30th, the last day of April.

31
00:02:29.167 --> 00:02:33.346
Seven days are left over, so
they go into May. It is 7 May.

32
00:02:33.846 --> 00:02:39.559
Time counts in sixties: 60 seconds,
60 minutes, and 24 hours in a day.

33
00:02:39.779 --> 00:02:43.680
The short hand gives the hour,
and each number is five minutes.

34
00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:48.497
After midday add 12, and a
timetable reads down and across.

35
00:02:48.717 --> 00:02:53.408
To find how long, count on in hops,
and days carry into the next month.
